meta {
  name: Update Settings
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/settings
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "values": {
      "MAX_ROWS": "5000",
      "APP_NAME": "D-Pilot",
      "AZURE_OPENAI_KEY": ""
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Save one or more settings; they apply without a restart. Each value is validated by kind (whole number, URL, boolean, JSON array, duration with a required unit and a value above zero, e.g. `24h`…; numbers are capped — `QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS` ≤ 2147483647, `MAX_ROWS`/`MCP_MAX_ROWS` ≤ 1000000, `SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL_HOURS` ≤ 8760, `LOGIN_TAGLINE`/`LOGIN_SUBTITLE` at most 80 characters (saving "" hides that login-page line), `LOGO_URL`/`LIGHT_LOGO_URL`/`FAVICON_URL` must be empty, a root-relative "/path" or an http(s) URL — never relative, protocol-relative ("//host") or containing a backslash, whitespace or control character ("LOGO_URL must be empty, a path on this server or an http(s) URL — use /path or https://…"), or lower where the operator set a `<KEY>_LIMIT` ceiling: "MAX_ROWS must be ≤ 500 (set by the server operator)") — the first invalid one is a 400 and nothing is written. An empty secret keeps the stored one. Changing `AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT` or `AZURE_OPENAI_INSECURE_TLS` while a key is stored requires `AZURE_OPENAI_KEY` in the same request (400 otherwise), so the key cannot be redirected. Changing `QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS` retires all pools without waiting — new queries get fresh pools, in-flight ones finish on the old pools, which close in the background; changing `SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL_HOURS` clears the schema cache. Audited as `SETTINGS_UPDATE` (key names only). Returns the same list as List Settings.

  Requires a valid Bearer JWT ({{token}}).
  **Admin only.**
}
